[category  Networking]
[require Tcl "8.5 9"]
[require spf [opt [vset SPF_VERSION]]]
[description]
[para]

The [package spf] package checks whether a host may send mail for a
domain according to the Sender Policy Framework (SPF) record of that
domain (1). A mail server can use it to check the address of a
connecting client against the domain of the envelope sender.

[para]

The SPF record is looked up as a TXT record of the domain. All DNS
queries are made with the [package dns] package, so the name server,
port, protocol and timeout are those set with [cmd ::dns::configure].
The commands wait for the answers, processing events meanwhile.

[section COMMANDS]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::spf::spf] [arg ip] [arg domain] [arg sender]]

Evaluates the SPF record of [arg domain] for a mail from [arg sender]
sent by the host with the IPv4 or IPv6 address [arg ip], and returns
the result:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const +]] Pass: the host is allowed to send mail for the domain.
[def [const -]] Fail: the host is not allowed to send mail for the domain.
[def [const ~]] Soft fail: the host is probably not allowed.
[def [const ?]] Neutral: the domain makes no statement about the host.
   This is also the result if no mechanism of the record matches.
[def [const none]] The domain has no SPF record.
[def [const unknown]] The record contains a mechanism that is not
   supported (see below).
[list_end]

If [arg domain] does not exist, the result is a list of two elements:
[const -] and the explanation [const {Domain Does Not Exist}].

[para]

[arg sender] is the envelope sender address. If it has no local part
(no [const @]), [const postmaster@] is put in front of it. [arg domain]
is the domain whose record is checked, usually the domain part of
[arg sender].

[para]

An error with the error code [const permanent] is thrown if
[arg sender] is empty or the record is not of version 1; an error with
the error code [const temporary] is thrown if the name server reports
a failure for the record lookup. A failure reported by the name server
while evaluating an [const include], [const a], [const mx] or
[const exists] mechanism or a [const redirect] modifier throws an error
[const {error in "}][arg mechanism][const {": }][arg message]. A domain
that does not exist counts as a domain without records. Failed lookups
in a [const ptr] mechanism only make it not match.

[list_end]

[section {SPF RECORDS}]

A record has the form [const v=spf1] followed by space-separated
mechanisms and modifiers. The mechanisms are evaluated from left to
right. Each mechanism may be preceded by a qualifier: [const +] (the
default), [const -], [const ~] or [const ?]. The first mechanism that
matches gives the result: its qualifier.

[para]

These mechanisms are supported:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const all]] Always matches.
[def "[const include:][arg domain]"] Matches if the record of
   [arg domain] gives the result [const +] for the host. Domains already
   visited are skipped.
[def "[const a][opt [const :][arg domain]][opt [const /][arg bits]]"]
   Matches if the host has one of the addresses of [arg domain]
   (default: the domain being checked): its IPv4 addresses for an IPv4
   host, its IPv6 addresses for an IPv6 host. With [arg bits], the host
   only needs to be in the network of that prefix length around an
   address.
[def "[const mx][opt [const :][arg domain]][opt [const /][arg bits]]"]
   Like [const a], for the addresses of the mail exchangers of
   [arg domain].
[def "[const ptr][opt [const :][arg domain]]"]
   Matches if the host has a name (found by a reverse lookup and
   confirmed by a forward lookup) that ends with [arg domain]
   (default: the domain being checked).
[def "[const ip4:][arg network][opt [const /][arg bits]]"]
[def "[const ip6:][arg network][opt [const /][arg bits]]"]
   Matches if the address of the host is in the given network. Without
   [arg bits] the address must be equal. An address of the other
   family never matches.
[def "[const exists:][arg domain]"]
   Matches if [arg domain] has an address record.
[list_end]

The [const redirect=][arg domain] modifier is used only if no mechanism
of the record matches: the result is then the result for the record of
[arg domain], or [const none] if [arg domain] has no SPF record. The
[const exp] and [const accredit] modifiers and unknown modifiers are
ignored.

[para]

Domain arguments may contain macros of the form
[const %{][arg letter][opt [arg digits]][opt [const r]][opt [arg delimiters]][const \}]:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const s]] The sender address.
[def [const l]] The local part of the sender address.
[def [const o]] The domain part of the sender address.
[def "[const d], [const h]"] The domain being checked.
[def [const i]] The IP address of the host; for IPv6, its hexadecimal
   digits separated by dots.
[def [const c]] The IP address of the host in readable form.
[def [const v]] [const in-addr] for an IPv4 address, [const ip6] for
   an IPv6 address.
[def [const r]] The name of the host performing the check.
[def [const t]] The current time in seconds.
[list_end]

The value is split at the [arg delimiters] (default [const .]), the
parts are reversed if [const r] is given, only the rightmost
[arg digits] parts are kept, and they are joined with dots. With an
upper-case letter the value is URL-encoded. [const %%], [const %_] and
[const %-] stand for a percent sign, a space and [const %20].

